        },
        {
          "id": "phil-bg4-no-trade-e5",
          "front": {
            "text": "Black has played 3...Nc6 and the e5-pawn is there for the taking. In this central configuration (e4 & d4 against e5 & d6), why is taking on e5 not as promising as it looks?",
            "boards": [
              {
                "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1",
                "orientation": "w",
                "moves": [
                  "e4",
                  "e5",
                  "Nf3",
                  "d6",
                  "d4",
                  "Nc6",
                  "dxe5",
                  "dxe5",
                  "Qxd8+",
                  "Kxd8"
                ],
                "solutionFrom": 6
              }
            ]
          },
          "back": {
            "text": "C: Black's knight covers e5, so the trade wins nothing there — with no knight on c6 the same sequence ends [3...Bg4 4.dxe5 dxe5 5.Qxd8+ Kxd8 | 6.Nxe5], a clean pawn (that is the [3...Bg4]? line).\n\nWe trade a more active pawn for a passive one and therefore help Black to gain scope for their pieces, in particular the king's bishop, which is limited by the d6-pawn.\n\nBlack has lost castling rights, but this is less of an issue once the queens are traded."
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "phil-bg4-refute",
          "front": {
            "text": "What do you play and why?",
            "boards": [
              {
                "fen": "rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1",
